|> Section
    handle=_ch6_start_

    *Notation.*
    We write

    $$
    \Large f : [\te{A}] \ra [\te{B}]
    $$

    to indicate that $f$ is a function $f : \rr \ra \rr$
    that takes inputs with units A
    and produces outputs with units B. 
    For example,

    $$
    \Large h : [\te{kg}] \ra [^\circ\te{F}]
    $$

    indicates that $h$ 
    is a function whose inputs denote kilograms and whose
    outputs denote degrees fahrenheit, etc.
    (These are purely “physical” concepts, not
    mathematical, but we're cool with that.)

    |> Pause

    *Vocabulary.* The input and output units of a function,
    when present as above, are referred to as the

    __signature__

    of the function.

|> Section
    *Compatible signatures for function operations.* 
    Taking the sum or product of functions etc
    presupposes some “compatibility” between their
    signatures—for 

|> Section
    *Function signatures.* 
    In some real-world
    scenarios the inputs and outputs of a function
    come with certain units attached. In this case,
    we write
    !! the numbers fed to a function may come
    !! with a certain type of unit attached, and 
    !! those output by the function as well; in this context, we
    !! write

    $$
    \Large f : [\te{A}] \ra [\te{B}]
    $$

    to indicate that the 
    units of inputs to $f$ are A while the units
    of numbers output by $f$ are B. 
    !! former and latter units
    !! are respectively A and B.
    !! i.e., the above indicates that 
    !! $f$ is a function $f : \rr \ra \rr$
    !! that takes inputs with units A
    !! and produces outputs with units B. 
    For example,

    $$
    \Large h : [\te{kg}] \ra [^\circ\te{F}]
    $$

    indicates that $h$ 
    is a function whose inputs denote kilograms and whose
    outputs denote degrees fahrenheit!

    |> Pause

    _Vocabulary_. We refer to this notation or
    to the ordered pair $(\te{A}, \te{B})$ as the

    __signature__

    of the function. (Thus a function signature has
    two parts: the units of the input, and the units
    of the output!)
    !! (Or “function signature”.) (Well.) 

    !! To put a fine point on it, a function signature
    !! has two parts: the units of the input (PART 1), and the units
    !! of the output (PART 2)!

    |> Pause

    Note that when we take the sum...

    $$
    \Large f + g
    $$

    ...of two functions, the same input, when evaluating the
    sum, is fed to both functions:

    $$
    \Large (f + g)(x) = f(x) + g(x).
    $$

    This means that in order for the sum to be make sense at
    all, the functions must take inputs with the same
    units; in addition, because apples should not be added
    to oranges, the two functions must output values with the
    same units as well; in other words, if $f$ and $g$ have
    signatures...

    $$
    \Large
    \begin{gathered}
    f : [\te{A}] \ra [\te{B}] \\
    g : [\te{C}] \ra [\te{D}] \up{1.35}
    \end{gathered}
    $$

    ...then taking the sum...

    $$
    \Large f + g
    $$

    ...implies the constraints...

    $$
    \Large
    \begin{aligned}
    \te{A} &= \te{C} \\
    \te{B} &= \te{D} \up{1.35}
    \end{aligned}
    $$

    ...on the units in order for the sum to be “well-formed”, not
    nonsensical!; i.e., the most general signature under which
    a sum of $f$ and $g$ makes sense is...

    $$
    \Large
    \begin{aligned}
    f : [\te{A}] \ra [\te{B}]& \\
    g : [\te{A}] \ra [\te{B}]& \up{1.35}
    \end{aligned}
    $$

    ...with the _same_ input units, and the _same_ output
    units!





